    word_icons=['bell', 'cross', 'check', 'switch', 'people'],
    words_note='Five words for today. A false positive is a false alarm. A false negative is a miss. Accuracy is the share of all cases a system gets right, and today shows how it can mislead. The threshold is the cut-off from Session 2. And bias is when the mistakes fall more often on one group. We start from the cut-off you met last time.',
    cover_note='Welcome back. In Session 2 a model learned from past records, gave each new case a score, and someone in your organisation set the cut-off. Today we ask whether such a system is any good. Every vendor will tell you a number, often ninety-five per cent accurate, so our question is this: how do I judge a vendor who says 95% accurate, and what will its mistakes cost us? We start with Kenya’s health insurer, build the idea with pictures of people and counts, tour six industries, and price the mistakes of a recorded model in shillings.',
    outcome_note='Today you learn to name the two ways a system can be wrong, and say why ninety-five per cent can be useless when the thing you look for is rare. You will treat the cut-off as a business dial and set it by pricing each mistake in shillings. And you will hold the questions that test a vendor’s claim, with a cost-of-a-mistake line on each entry in your portfolio.',
